
---
import Navigation from "../../components/navigation.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/posts.js";

const post = posts.find((post) => post.slug === "post_sixteen");

if (!post) {
  throw new Error("Post not found: post_sixteen");
}

const { title, date, dateTime, tags } = post;

const tagSlug = (tag) =>
  tag.toLowerCase().trim().replaceAll(" ", "-");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>{title} | Ivy Duncan</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Why I called my romance books the Proximity Series, how the trope connects the books, and the other series I have planned."
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Navigation />

    <main class="container">
      <article class="blog-post narrow">
      <a href="/blog" class="back-link">&larr; Back to the blog</a>
        <header class="post-header">


          <h1>{title}</h1>

          <p class="post-date">
            <time datetime={dateTime}>{date}</time>
          </p>

          {tags.length > 0 && (
            <div class="post-tags">
              {tags.map((tag) => (
                <a
                  href={`/blog/tag/${tagSlug(tag)}`}
                  class="post-tag"
                >
                  {tag}
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
        </header>

        <div class="post-content">
          <h2>Why Is the Series Called the Proximity Series?</h2>

          <p>
            This is kind of stupid, but I really hate coming up with book
            names. And I hate coming up with series names even more.
          </p>

          <p>
            The name <em>Proximity</em> came from the fact that all the books
            in the series have a proximity trope in some way.
          </p>

          <p>Here's how it works for each book:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Books One and Two:</strong> The characters are
              neighbors who are forced into each other's lives because
              their respective roommates are dating each other.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Book Three:</strong> The characters are in the same
              class and sit right next to each other. They also frequent the
              same places on campus, including the library and coffee shop.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Book Four:</strong> <em>(Spoilers!)</em> Roommates.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Now, this is kind of a stupid reason to name a series, because
            when you think about it, most romance books have some kind of
            proximity trope in them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For example, <em>Five Years and 2,000 Miles</em> has Sloan and
            Leo in a class together. So, technically, that has proximity,
            too.
          </p>

          <p>
            But I don't know what else I would have called the series, so
            <em>Proximity</em> it is!
          </p>

          <h2>What's Next?</h2>

          <p>
            I have two more series planned for after the Proximity Series
            concludes.
          </p>

          <p>
            One I'm not ready to talk about publicly yet, but that one
            already has a series name picked out. So at least I managed to
            make that decision ahead of time!
          </p>

          <p>
            The other has a reconnection theme. I have three stories planned
            about couples who knew each other when they were younger and
            then reconnected as adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            I haven't figured out what I'm going to call that series yet.
            Apparently, coming up with series names is going to remain a
            problem for me.
          </p>
        

        <div class="post-footer">
            <a href="/blog" class="button button-secondary">
              Back to Blog
            </a>
          </div>
          </div>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .container {
    width: min(1100px, calc(100% - 3rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .blog-post {
    padding: 5rem 0;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    color: #765f48;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .post-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .post-header h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  .post-date {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    color: #6f6a63;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .post-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .post-tag {
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .post-tag:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .post-content {
    color: #3f3a35;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .post-content h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  .post-content p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content strong {
    color: #765f48;
  }

  .post-content ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .post-footer {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
  }

  .button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.85rem 1.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid #765f48;
    transition:
      background-color 0.2s ease,
      color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .button-secondary {
    color: #765f48;
    background: transparent;
  }

  .button-secondary:hover {
    color: #fff;
    background: #765f48;
  }

  .site-footer {
    padding: 2rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
    color: #6f6a63;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .container {
      width: min(100% - 2rem, 1100px);
    }

    .blog-post {
      padding: 3.5rem 0;
    }
  }
</style>